\documentclass{article}
\title{Who Joins a Community Garden?\\A Survey of Four Neighbourhood Sites}
\author{P.~Ndlovu}
\date{May 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Background}
Community gardens in the city have doubled in number since 2018,
and most now keep a \textb{waiting list} of would-be members.
Who ends up on these lists, and who actually gets a plot,
has so far escaped systematic study.

We surveyed members and applicants at four garden sites
between March and May 2026.
The questionnaire covered gardening experience,
household composition, motivation, and distance from home to the site.

\section{Main Findings}
Three patterns recur across all four sites:
\begin{enumerate}
\item members with prior gardening experience
are admitted from the waiting list about twice as fast;
\item households within a ten-minute walk
make up most of the active membership;
\item applicants who joined a working bee before applying
are rarely turned away.

\section{Discussion}
The admission advantage of experienced gardeners
suggests that informal skill signals
shape who gets access to scarce plots.
Working bees double as a screening device,
which may deter applicants with less free time.

\section{Next Steps}
A follow-up wave in the autumn will revisit the same sites
and interview applicants who withdrew from the waiting lists.
\end{document}

